namespace Pathway.Content;

static partial class Curriculum
{
    // Theoretical content lives in the original curriculum. This companion expands
    // the lessons with physical explanations, worked designs, and safer bench tasks.
    // Presentation exercises here are study/practice notes, not immediate quizzes.
    private static Exercise StudyExercise(string title, string prompt, string[] checkpoints) =>
        new(ExerciseKind.Presentation, title, prompt, checkpoints, null, null, [], "Review the design and work through the checkpoints.", []);

    private static Lesson EnrichElectricalLesson(Lesson lesson)
    {
        var additionalMaterial = lesson.Slug switch
        {
            "ee-charge-voltage-current" => "Physical meaning: charge is measured in coulombs (C). One ampere (A) means one coulomb of charge passes a cross-section each second. One volt (V) is one joule of energy per coulomb of charge: voltage compares the energy available per unit charge between two points; it is not a count of electrons. A voltmeter reads a difference, so identify both probe points. When a wire closes a circuit, electromagnetic fields establish the energy-transfer conditions very quickly even though individual electrons in metal usually drift slowly. The electrons already occupy the wire; they do not have to travel from the battery to the lamp before it responds.\n\nTry a 3 V battery pack and a suitable LED with a series resistor. Measure about 3 V across the disconnected source, then close the switch and observe current through the complete circuit. The source can show voltage with the switch open even when current is zero. The 'water pressure' analogy can help with voltage, but electricity includes electric fields, charge movement, energy and time-dependent behavior that the analogy cannot fully capture.",
            "ee-resistance-circuits" => "A resistor is a component intentionally designed to oppose charge motion and dissipate electrical energy as heat. Resistance is measured in ohms (Ω): 1 Ω means a 1 V difference produces 1 A through an ideal ohmic element. In metal wires, resistance depends on material, length, cross-sectional area and temperature. Contacts and corroded connectors can add unintended resistance too. A short circuit is an unwanted near-zero-resistance path; it may bypass a load and draw a dangerous current limited mostly by the source and wires.\n\nBench experiment: with a disconnected 1 kΩ resistor, measure resistance directly across its two leads; then try a 10 kΩ resistor. A resistance reading needs test current: the meter's Ω mode supplies a small internal measurement current. Measure with external power removed and account for other parallel paths, which can make an in-circuit result inaccurate. An OL display may indicate an open path, an over-range reading, or some components that do not behave like ordinary resistors.",
            "ee-ohms-law" => "Worked example: a 9 V battery applied to a 1 kΩ resistor produces I = 9/1000 = 0.009 A = 9 mA, assuming a reasonably stable 9 V at the resistor. The resistor dissipates P = VI = 0.081 W, so a standard 0.25 W part has headroom in this example. If the voltage doubles while resistance stays the same, current doubles. If resistance doubles with voltage fixed, current halves.\n\nUse Ohm's law with the voltage across the *specific component*, not automatically the entire supply voltage. LED junctions, batteries, motors and semiconductor switches are not accurately modeled as fixed resistors across all operating conditions. A useful workflow is to label every component, estimate voltage across it, calculate expected current, then check ratings and verify with a meter.",
            "ee-unit-conversion" => "Engineering prefixes: micro (µ) = 0.000001, milli (m) = 0.001, kilo (k) = 1000, and mega (M) = 1,000,000. Letter case matters: mA means milliamps, MA means megaamps. A 470 Ω resistor is 0.47 kΩ; 0.02 A is 20 mA; 100 µF is 0.0001 F. When calculating R = V/I, convert milliamps to amps first or track units explicitly.\n\nRead a multimeter's unit indicator before concluding that a 1.0 reading means 1 Ω: it might be 1.0 kΩ or 1.0 MΩ. 'OL' is not 0 Ω; it usually means an open path or beyond the meter's range. Voltage, resistance and current are different physical quantities, not interchangeable meter settings.",
            "ee-series-parallel" => "Series means components share one path and therefore the same branch current. Their voltage drops add to the supply voltage. Parallel means components connect to the same two nodes and therefore see the same voltage; total current is the sum of branch currents. For a 12 V source and 1 kΩ + 2 kΩ in series: total 3 kΩ, current 4 mA, voltage drops 4 V and 8 V. For the same resistors in parallel across 12 V: currents are 12 mA and 6 mA; total is 18 mA.\n\nCheck your physical wiring rather than trusting appearance: two components next to each other on a breadboard may or may not share a node. Never place an ammeter directly across the source to 'see total current'; its low internal resistance may short the source.",
            "ee-voltage-divider" => "Use a divider to create a reference voltage for a light-duty input, not as a general substitute for a power regulator. Example: with R1 = 10 kΩ from 5 V to the midpoint and R2 = 10 kΩ from midpoint to 0 V, the no-load output is 2.5 V. Divider current is 5 V / 20 kΩ = 0.25 mA. If you attach a load to the midpoint, it sits in parallel with R2 and changes the effective resistance and output voltage.\n\nFor a switch sensor, a fixed pull-up resistor can create a default high voltage, while a pushbutton to ground creates a low voltage. For real inputs, compare the resulting voltages with the microcontroller's guaranteed input thresholds and ensure no pin ever exceeds its supply-voltage limits.",
            "ee-power-energy" => "Watts describe the rate of energy transfer; watt-hours measure energy accumulated over time. A 12 V circuit drawing 0.5 A uses 6 W. Operated for 2 hours, it consumes 12 Wh (ignoring source conversion losses). A 1 kΩ resistor with 5 V across it dissipates 25 mW, while a 100 Ω resistor at 5 V dissipates 250 mW, right at the nominal rating of a 0.25 W resistor: choose margin rather than operating continuously at its rating.\n\nA power adapter labeled 12 V, 2 A normally *offers up to* 2 A; a compatible 12 V device needing 0.5 A draws roughly 0.5 A. It does not automatically receive 2 A. Verify AC versus DC, polarity, regulated voltage, connector fit, and whether the load has high startup/inrush demand.",
            "ee-kirchhoff" => "Kirchhoff's Current Law (KCL) makes sense when you remember charge cannot accumulate indefinitely at a junction in a steady-state circuit. For example, if a 20 mA supply splits into an LED branch drawing 12 mA, the other branch draws 8 mA. Kirchhoff's Voltage Law (KVL) tracks energy per charge: in a 9 V series loop with 2 V across an LED, approximately 7 V must appear across its resistor and any other series elements together.\n\nMark a plus/minus polarity across each element and choose a loop direction consistently. Negative results can mean the actual current or voltage has the opposite direction from your chosen reference; they are not automatically calculation mistakes.",
            "ee-schematics" => "Start at the power source, follow each conductive node, locate branch points and return to the source. A crossing without a junction dot is generally not a connected node; always consult the drawing convention. Reference designators identify parts: R1 is a resistor, C1 a capacitor, D1 a diode, SW1 a switch, and J1 a connector. Values and labels specify what to buy and where it connects.\n\nSketch an LED circuit before wiring: regulated +5 V → R1 (330 Ω) → D1 LED anode → LED cathode → 0 V. The LED's symbol has a cathode bar; most physical LEDs have a cathode indicator (often a short lead or flat side), but check the datasheet. The schematic shows connections, not where the part physically goes on a breadboard.",
            "ee-multimeter" => "Set the dial and lead jacks before touching the circuit. Black lead normally goes to COM. For voltage and resistance, red lead usually goes to V/Ω. Voltage uses a high-impedance parallel measurement on a powered circuit; current typically requires opening the circuit and inserting the meter in series using its current input. A meter left in current mode can short a source if placed across two powered nodes.\n\nFor resistance/continuity/diode/capacitance: remove external power, isolate the component where appropriate, and safely discharge capacitors. In resistance mode the meter's battery supplies a small test signal. Diode-test mode reports forward voltage (often ~0.5–0.8 V for ordinary silicon at the meter's test current); an open/OL reading in one polarity is not a complete proof of a diode until you reverse the probes and confirm. Measure only isolated low-voltage practice circuits; household mains and mains-powered equipment need training and suitably rated instruments.",
            "ee-capacitors-rc" => "Two conductive plates separated by an insulator form a capacitor. Capacitance C in farads means charge storage per voltage (Q = CV). The dielectric is the insulating material between the plates. The plates do not pass a continuous DC conduction current through the ideal dielectric; instead the capacitor charges or discharges as the surrounding circuit changes. After a long time across a steady DC supply, an ideal capacitor behaves like an open circuit.\n\nWith R = 10 kΩ and C = 100 µF, τ = RC = 1 second. A charging capacitor rises to about 63% of its final voltage after 1τ, 86% after 2τ, and 95% after 3τ; discharging falls to about 37%, 14%, and 5%. Real components have tolerance, leakage, equivalent series resistance (ESR) and voltage ratings. Check polarity on electrolytic capacitors and never exceed their ratings.",
            "ee-inductors" => "Current through an inductor changes with the voltage across it according to V = L × (di/dt). A switching motor or relay coil tries to keep its current flowing briefly after its power path opens, producing a potentially damaging voltage spike. A diode placed in reverse polarity across an ordinary DC relay coil is normally off during powered operation but provides a current path when the switch opens.\n\nChoose a flyback diode according to the coil supply, current and switching requirements, and follow the relay/module documentation. The clamp usually slows relay release; faster release may require a different suppression network. Never assume AC coils or motors accept the same simple diode arrangement.",
            "ee-ac-fundamentals" => "Direct current (DC) means current flowing with one overall direction in the chosen reference, though its magnitude can vary. Alternating current (AC) reverses direction periodically; in a 60 Hz sine wave there are 60 full cycles each second. In metal, electrons move back and forth about their mean position rather than traversing the entire household wiring run each half-cycle. A larger AC voltage means a larger electric potential difference, not a higher frequency.\n\nFor an ideal sine wave, RMS voltage = peak voltage / √2. Thus 120 V RMS mains has a peak magnitude of about 170 V; 240 V RMS is about 340 V peak. These are hazardous voltages, not suitable for beginner experiments. Use a low-voltage function generator or an isolated low-voltage AC source when learning waveform measurement.",
            "ee-semiconductors" => "A diode has an anode and a cathode. Under forward bias a suitable diode conducts with a voltage drop; under reverse bias it ordinarily blocks current up to its breakdown rating. A standard rectifier diode can protect against some reverse-polarity situations; a Schottky diode generally has a lower forward voltage and different leakage/reverse-voltage limits. A light-emitting diode (LED) emits light when forward current flows and needs a current-limiting element.\n\nA bipolar junction transistor (BJT) and a metal-oxide-semiconductor field-effect transistor (MOSFET) are different kinds of electronic switches. The transistor's datasheet determines safe voltage, current, drive and heat dissipation. A MOSFET gate can require a specific voltage relative to its source to reach low on-resistance; a quoted threshold voltage is not a guarantee of full-on switching.",
            "ee-digital-electrical" => "A general-purpose input/output (GPIO) pin is a digital device connection that can read or drive a logic level within its rated voltage/current limits. A floating input is not guaranteed high or low. A pull-up resistor establishes a weak default high level; a normally-open switch can short the input to 0 V when pressed, with the pull-up limiting current. Many microcontrollers provide internal pull-ups; confirm active-low behavior in code.\n\nA 'dry contact' is an electrically isolated switch contact rather than a voltage output. Use a relay or other suitably isolated interface when you need to mimic a dry contact from a microcontroller. Never wire an unknown wall-control line directly to GPIO: measure and verify the interface first.",
            "ee-engineering-habits" => "Before purchasing, write down: supply type/voltage, expected peak and steady currents, connector pinout, switch state, component tolerance, and the measurements that would prove the design works. Draw a labeled schematic and keep a table of expected-versus-observed voltages. If an LED is dark, first confirm 5 V exists at the source, measure the resistor and LED voltages, and inspect polarity before swapping parts.\n\nKeep a safe build log, photos and component datasheet links. Use current-limited low-voltage supplies. Never defeat safety interlocks on motors, doors, heating equipment or other mechanisms simply to make a project function.",
            _ => string.Empty
        };
        var module = lesson.Order <= 10 ? "Electrical foundations and measurements"
            : lesson.Order <= 14 ? "Components and practical circuits"
            : "Control interfaces and build projects";

        // Short, immediate multiple-choice questions are replaced by instructional
        // notes; module assessments occur after substantial study material.
        var exercise = lesson.Exercise.Kind == ExerciseKind.MultipleChoice
            ? StudyExercise("Worked-example notebook", "Trace the example and explain the observed signal before continuing.",
                ["Identify the source and reference node", "Predict the current or voltage before using the meter", "Check component ratings and polarity"])
            : lesson.Exercise;

        return lesson with
        {
            Order = lesson.Order * 100,
            Module = module,
            Body = lesson.Body + "\n\n" + additionalMaterial,
            Exercise = exercise
        };
    }

    private static IEnumerable<Lesson> BuildPracticalElectricalLessons() =>
        new Lesson[]
        {
            new("ee-physical-quantities-lab",
                "Electrical foundations and measurements",
                150,
                "Voltage, current and resistance in the physical world",
                "Turn volts, amps, ohms and watts into measurable properties of a real circuit.",
                "Voltage is energy per charge; current is charge per second; resistance relates the two.",
                "A coulomb is an amount of electric charge. In a metal conductor, a huge population of electrons already occupies the material. An ampere tells how much charge crosses one location each second; 1 A = 1 C/s. A volt describes the electrical potential difference: 1 V = 1 joule per coulomb (J/C). A resistor converts electrical energy into heat while restricting current. A watt describes how quickly energy is transferred: 1 W = 1 J/s.\n\nThink of voltage as a difference between two points, not a property of only one wire. A battery can maintain a 9 V difference across an open switch when no charge is flowing around the whole loop. Once the loop closes, a current can circulate through the lamp/resistor and back to the source. For a 9 V source with a 1 kΩ load, an approximate 9 mA current flows; the resistor consumes 81 mW.\n\nPractical exercise: on an isolated battery-powered breadboard, measure battery voltage while the switch is open. Close the switch and measure voltage across the resistor. Remove power and measure the isolated resistor in ohms mode. The meter creates its own test current in Ω mode. Record what changes and what stays constant. Never put the meter in current mode across the battery terminals.",
                "9 V source -> switch -> R1 1 kΩ -> 0 V\nI = V / R = 9 / 1000 = 0.009 A = 9 mA\nP = V x I = 9 x 0.009 = 0.081 W",
                StudyExercise("Design notebook", "Use these practical checkpoints before moving to the next build or module.", new[] { "Recognize the source and return path", "State where voltage is measured", "Predict zero current with an open switch" }),
                null,
                ElectricalFoundationsDocs),
            new("ee-dc-vs-ac-motion",
                "Electrical foundations and measurements",
                250,
                "What electrons do in DC and AC circuits",
                "Understand electron drift, current direction, voltage, frequency and waveform.",
                "DC and AC describe signal direction over time, not separate kinds of electrons.",
                "In common metallic wiring, electrons are the mobile charge carriers. When a DC supply is connected to a closed conductive path, their average drift is toward the positive terminal; conventional current, a useful engineering convention defined before electron motion was understood, points the opposite way. Drift is much slower than propagation of changes in electric and magnetic fields, which is why a lamp responds promptly when you close a switch.\n\nIn AC, voltage and current vary with time and often reverse polarity. For a 60 Hz sine wave, one full cycle takes 1/60 second (~16.7 ms). Electrons in a wire oscillate back and forth over small distances while energy travels from the source to the load. Raising the RMS voltage from 120 V to 240 V increases voltage amplitude; it does not by itself change 60 Hz frequency. The actual current depends on the connected load and its resistance or impedance.\n\nUse a safe isolated low-voltage signal source or a simulator to view positive and negative half-cycles; do not experiment on mains. A rectifier diode can pass one polarity of current and block the other, turning AC into a pulsating one-direction current, typically followed by filtering and regulation.",
                "DC example: battery + -> load -> battery - (conventional current)\nAC example: 60 Hz = 60 complete cycles per second\nT = 1 / 60 Hz = 16.7 ms per cycle",
                StudyExercise("Design notebook", "Use these practical checkpoints before moving to the next build or module.", new[] { "Differentiate conventional current from electron drift", "Explain what AC frequency measures", "Do not use household mains as a lab source" }),
                null,
                ElectricalFoundationsDocs),
            new("ee-adapter-current-capacity",
                "Electrical foundations and measurements",
                380,
                "Power adapters: volts, amps and what a load draws",
                "Choose a compatible supply without confusing current capacity with forced current.",
                "A supply provides a voltage and a maximum safe current; a compatible load determines its draw.",
                "On a typical regulated 12 V DC adapter, a rating of 2 A means the supply is intended to provide up to about 2 A within its specifications. If a compatible piece of equipment normally draws 0.5 A, it does not automatically receive 2 A. Its own electrical characteristics determine current draw. The supply must still have enough capacity for startup peaks, and some adapters have minimum-load or unusual regulation requirements.\n\nCheck voltage first (12 V is not interchangeable with 5 V), then DC vs AC, connector size, center-positive versus center-negative polarity, maximum supply current, quality and the manufacturer's specifications. Estimate needed power from P = VI: a 12 V, 0.5 A device requires about 6 W while operating. A 12 V, 2 A adapter has a nominal 24 W output capacity, but that does not guarantee compatibility if polarity or connector is wrong.\n\nAt the workbench: read the adapter label; verify DC polarity and no-load voltage with the meter in V DC mode if safe; compare with the equipment label before connecting anything. Do not test current capacity by shorting the adapter with an ammeter.",
                "Required device: 12 V DC, 0.5 A, center positive\nCandidate supply: regulated 12 V DC, >= 0.5 A, center positive\nPower required: P = 12 V x 0.5 A = 6 W",
                StudyExercise("Design notebook", "Use these practical checkpoints before moving to the next build or module.", new[] { "Verify voltage and polarity before current rating", "Calculate device power", "Never short a source with ammeter leads" }),
                null,
                ElectricalFoundationsDocs),
            new("ee-resistors-real-designs",
                "Electrical foundations and measurements",
                550,
                "Resistors in actual designs: limiting, dividing and biasing",
                "Select resistor values and power ratings for LED, pull-up and sensor circuits.",
                "A resistor may limit current, set a voltage, create a default logic level or shape timing.",
                "Current limiting: an LED is not a fixed resistor. A series resistor absorbs the supply voltage not used by the LED and limits current. Suppose an LED has about a 2 V forward drop at 10 mA from a regulated 5 V source. R = (5-2)/0.010 = 300 Ω; choose a nearby standard 330 Ω part, giving about 9.1 mA. The resistor dissipates I²R ≈ 0.027 W, comfortably below a 0.25 W rating. LED forward voltage varies with model, current and temperature; use a datasheet rather than blindly assuming 2 V.\n\nVoltage divider: place R1 from supply to midpoint and R2 from midpoint to 0 V. With 10 kΩ each and 5 V applied, the unloaded midpoint is 2.5 V. A load on the midpoint changes the division, so it is normally intended for sensing or light-duty references, not running a motor.\n\nInput biasing: connect a 10 kΩ pull-up from a microcontroller's safe logic supply to its input; connect a switch from that input to ground. The input reads high when untouched and low when pressed. These are starting values, not universal requirements: choose resistance based on input leakage, noise, power budget and device datasheet.",
                "LED: +5 V -> 330 Ω -> LED anode -> LED cathode -> GND\nDivider: +5 V -> 10 kΩ -> Vout -> 10 kΩ -> GND\nPull-up: +3.3 V -> 10 kΩ -> input -> switch -> GND",
                StudyExercise("Design notebook", "Use these practical checkpoints before moving to the next build or module.", new[] { "Compute LED series resistor", "Identify voltage-divider loading", "Describe why a floating input needs a bias resistor" }),
                null,
                ElectricalFoundationsDocs),
            new("ee-breadboard-led-project",
                "Electrical foundations and measurements",
                960,
                "Build project: a current-limited LED indicator",
                "Wire, calculate, measure and diagnose an isolated low-voltage LED circuit.",
                "A good build starts with a drawing and predicted values, then verifies every connection.",
                "Parts: an isolated 3.3 V regulated low-voltage source or battery pack, ordinary red LED, 270 Ω resistor rated 0.25 W or more, momentary pushbutton, breadboard, hookup wire and multimeter. Check the LED datasheet; for this worked example we assume forward voltage near 2.0 V at a few mA. A 270 Ω resistor gives roughly (3.3-2.0)/270 = 0.0048 A = 4.8 mA. The resistor dissipates about 0.0063 W. Do not put an LED straight across the supply.\n\nStep 1: with supply disconnected, arrange a series path from +3.3 V through the normally-open switch, the 270 Ω resistor, LED anode and LED cathode to 0 V. Breadboard strips connect only certain holes; check the strip layout and rail breaks. Step 2: visually trace the exact electrical path and confirm LED polarity before turning on power. Step 3: power the circuit and press the button; the LED should light. Step 4: measure voltage across the supply, LED and resistor. The drops should add close to the supply voltage. Step 5: isolate power before measuring resistor resistance.\n\nTroubleshooting: a dark LED may mean reverse polarity, a missing breadboard connection, open switch, dead source or damaged LED. Verify supply and node voltages before replacing parts. The circuit is intentionally low energy; do not connect it to household wiring.",
                "+3.3 V -- SW1 -- R1 270 Ω --|>|-- 0 V\n                 red LED\nR ≈ (3.3 V - 2.0 V) / 0.005 A = 260 Ω\nStandard nearby part: 270 Ω",
                NumericExercise("Calculate and compare", "A 3.3 V source drives an LED dropping 2.0 V through 270 Ω. Estimate the LED current in milliamps.", 4.8, 0.2, "mA", "Write down the relationship and convert units before calculating.", "I = (3.3 - 2.0) / 270 = 0.00481 A = 4.81 mA."),
                null,
                ElectricalFoundationsDocs),
            new("ee-meter-component-identification",
                "Electrical foundations and measurements",
                1040,
                "Lab: distinguish a resistor, diode and capacitor with a meter",
                "Use ohms, diode-test and capacitance modes without mistaking OL for a diagnosis.",
                "Measure an isolated, unpowered component in the correct mode and both polarities.",
                "Preparation: obtain a known 1 kΩ resistor, a small signal diode (for example a 1N4148), and a low-voltage nonpolarized capacitor (for example 1 µF); check the parts' markings or datasheets. Disconnect all parts from any powered circuit. Capacitors can retain charge after power is removed: safely discharge and verify near 0 V before connecting the meter. Avoid high-voltage capacitors for this exercise.\n\nResistor: set Ω mode and place probes on the two leads. Read the actual prefix (Ω, kΩ or MΩ). Reverse the probes; an ordinary resistor reads approximately the same either way. Diode: select diode-test mode, red lead to anode and black to cathode. A small silicon signal diode commonly reads a forward drop around 0.5 to 0.8 V. Reverse the leads: normally OL. Diode-test results depend on the meter's test current and the component. Capacitor: a capacitance meter reads charge-storage capacity after suitable isolation/discharge. In ordinary ohms mode some capacitors momentarily show changing numbers as the meter charges them, then eventually OL; this alone cannot establish the capacitance value or prove the capacitor is healthy.\n\nMake a table: component, polarity, mode, expected reading, observed reading, uncertainty. If a part produces OL in both directions, consider an open device, poor contacts, an unsupported test current/range or circuit topology before declaring it defective. Never measure Ω on a powered switch connected to a controller.",
                "1 kΩ resistor: Ω -> ~1.0 kΩ either direction\nSmall silicon diode: diode mode -> ~0.6 V one way, OL reversed\n1 µF capacitor: capacitance mode -> near 1 µF (within tolerance)",
                StudyExercise("Design notebook", "Use these practical checkpoints before moving to the next build or module.", new[] { "Isolate and discharge before Ω/capacitance tests", "Swap diode probe directions", "Recognize that OL alone is ambiguous" }),
                null,
                ElectricalFoundationsDocs),
            new("ee-foundations-module-assessment",
                "Electrical foundations and measurements",
                1080,
                "Foundations assessment: predict the reading",
                "Apply units, current limiting and safe meter setup after the full foundations module.",
                "The correct meter connection follows what physical quantity you want to observe.",
                "Review the supply–load–return model, Ohm's law, series/parallel networks, power ratings and meter modes from the preceding lessons. Think about why voltage readings use probes across two nodes and why a current meter must become part of an existing current path. A resistor is measured with power off and isolated when parallel pathways might affect the result.\n\nIn a 5 V LED circuit containing a 330 Ω series resistor, you should expect a smaller voltage across the resistor than the source because the LED also has a forward drop. In a 10 kΩ / 10 kΩ unloaded divider across 5 V, the midpoint should be near 2.5 V. Perform each prediction on paper before checking a simulated or isolated low-voltage build.",
                "Voltage measurement: meter V DC across R1\nResistance measurement: isolate R1, power off, meter Ω\nCurrent measurement: insert meter in series, never across source",
                ChoiceExercise("Module assessment", "Which method safely measures an isolated 1 kΩ resistor?", "off", new Choice[] { new("off", "Turn external power off, isolate the resistor, measure across its leads in Ω mode"), new("live", "Leave the supply connected and measure across its leads in Ω mode"), new("amps", "Put the meter in A mode directly across the battery") }, "Resistance mode supplies its own test signal.", "Applies module understanding safely"),
                null,
                ElectricalFoundationsDocs),
            new("ee-capacitor-use-cases",
                "Components and practical circuits",
                1150,
                "Capacitors in real designs: filters, timing and decoupling",
                "Learn when to use capacitor behavior and how to select voltage, type and value.",
                "Capacitors store electric-field energy and oppose sudden voltage changes.",
                "A capacitor stores separated charge on two conductors with an insulating dielectric. Its capacitance is measured in farads. A capacitor connected directly across an ideal DC voltage source initially accepts a changing current as its voltage rises, then approaches zero DC current once charged. Real capacitors leak slightly. Capacitors are not batteries: their stored energy is ½CV² and they do not regulate voltage or provide unlimited current.\n\nCommon applications: (1) decoupling: connect a 100 nF ceramic capacitor close between a digital integrated circuit's Vcc and ground pins to supply brief high-frequency current; larger bulk capacitors may handle slower load steps. (2) timing: a resistor–capacitor (RC) pair creates a predictable charging curve, used for delays, reset timing and button debounce. (3) filtering: capacitors with resistors form frequency-dependent networks. (4) coupling: a capacitor may pass changing signals while blocking DC bias between analog stages. (5) smoothing: power converters may use capacitors to reduce ripple, with selection governed by ripple current and voltage.\n\nSelection process: choose nominal capacitance, operating voltage plus margin, dielectric or polarity, tolerance, temperature range and ESR. For instance, a 100 µF, 16 V electrolytic could be suitable on a well-behaved 5 V supply when its datasheet and circuit conditions agree, but incorrect polarity can cause failure. Keep experiments on small low-voltage capacitors.",
                "Decoupling:\nVcc ---+---- IC Vcc\n       |\n      100 nF\n       |\n0 V ---+---- IC GND\n\nRC delay: +5 V -- 10 kΩ --+-- node\n                         |\n                       100 µF\n                         |\n                        0 V",
                StudyExercise("Design notebook", "Use these practical checkpoints before moving to the next build or module.", new[] { "Contrast decoupling with bulk energy storage", "Explain RC timing", "Check capacitor voltage and polarity ratings" }),
                null,
                ElectricalFoundationsDocs),
            new("ee-switch-debounce-cap-project",
                "Components and practical circuits",
                1170,
                "Build project: RC timing and button debouncing",
                "Build a resistor–capacitor network and observe a timed voltage change.",
                "RC charging follows a time constant; a mechanical contact can create noisy transitions.",
                "A mechanical switch rarely moves between perfect open and closed states instantly; contact bounce can cause multiple rapid electrical transitions. A simple RC low-pass network smooths changes at a node, but the digital input still needs properly defined logic thresholds, ideally a Schmitt-trigger input or suitable debouncing code. A Schmitt trigger changes state at different rising and falling voltage thresholds, reducing sensitivity to small noise around the threshold.\n\nPrototype an unpowered 10 kΩ resistor and 100 µF low-voltage capacitor in series between a controlled 5 V supply and 0 V, with the capacitor from midpoint to ground. Confirm capacitor polarity (if electrolytic) and check expected τ = R×C = 1 s. Energize through the resistor and watch midpoint voltage rise on the meter; about 63% of the final value is expected after roughly 1 second, with normal component tolerances. Remove supply and discharge safely through an appropriate resistor (not by shorting large capacitors).\n\nFor a real button debounce design, a capacitor across a switch or input may affect the source when contacts close, so use a current-limiting resistor and review the input's absolute maximum ratings. Do not attach an RC network to an unidentified commercial controller line without knowing the protocol.",
                "+5 V -- 10 kΩ --+-- meter red\n                |\n              100 µF (rated >5 V)\n                |\n0 V ------------+-- meter black\nτ = 10,000 Ω x 0.0001 F = 1 second",
                NumericExercise("Calculate and compare", "For a 5 V source and a charging capacitor starting at 0 V, what voltage is expected after one RC time constant?", 3.16, 0.15, "V", "Write down the relationship and convert units before calculating.", "After one time constant the voltage reaches 63.2% of final: 5 × 0.632 = 3.16 V."),
                null,
                ElectricalFoundationsDocs),
            new("ee-wave-properties", "Components and practical circuits", 1320, "Wave anatomy: amplitude and period", "Read wave height and the duration of one cycle.", "Amplitude sets the vertical size; period is time per complete cycle; frequency is cycles per second.", "## Learn the idea\nA **waveform** is a graph of a quantity changing over time. A **cycle** is one complete repeat. The **amplitude** A is the greatest displacement from the centerline; it is not the full top-to-bottom height (which is 2A). The **period** T is the duration of a cycle, measured in seconds. The **frequency** f is cycles per second (hertz, Hz), so f = 1/T. Example: T = 0.25 s means f = 4 Hz.\n\n## Where you'll use it\nAn audio tone repeats its pressure variation thousands of times per second; a power-system AC signal repeats 50 or 60 times per second depending on the supply. We only explore these in the simulator or isolated safe low-voltage circuitry.\n\n## Walk through one problem\n1. A low-voltage waveform reaches +3 V and −3 V about a zero centerline. Its amplitude is **3 V**, not 6 V.\n2. Adjacent peaks are 0.2 s apart, so one period is **0.2 s**.\n3. Frequency is 1/0.2 = **5 Hz**. Check: five periods fit into one second.\n\n## Explore\nUse the interactive waveform below. Change amplitude without changing period, then change period without changing amplitude. Observe the changes directly; the numeric question below checks that you can calculate frequency.\n\n## Watch out\nWave height and amplitude are different: a ±3 V signal has 6 V peak-to-peak but a 3 V amplitude.", "amplitude = 3 V ; peak-to-peak = 6 V\nT = 0.2 s ; f = 1/T = 5 Hz", NumericExercise("Wave practice", "A periodic low-voltage waveform completes one cycle in 0.25 seconds. What is the frequency in hertz?", 4d, 0.001d, "Hz", "Use the worked steps and keep the sign and units.", "One cycle lasts T = 0.25 s. Frequency f = 1/T = 1/0.25 = 4 Hz."), null, ElectricalFoundationsDocs),
            new("ee-wave-phase", "Components and practical circuits", 1330, "Phase: shifting a wave left or right", "See how two identical-frequency signals can be out of step.", "Phase is a position within a cycle; changing phase shifts the wave in time without changing its period or amplitude.", "## Learn the idea\nTwo sine waves can have the same **amplitude** and **frequency** but reach their peaks at different times. Their difference in cycle position is called **phase**, usually expressed in degrees. A full cycle is 360°, a quarter-cycle is 90°, and half a cycle is 180°. The formula v(t) = A sin(2πt/T + φ) uses: v(t), the instantaneous voltage; t, time in seconds; A, peak amplitude in volts; T, period in seconds; φ (phi), phase angle in **radians** in this formula. A 90° shift equals π/2 radians.\n\n## Where you'll use it\nIn audio, phase affects whether two microphones reinforce or cancel certain frequencies. In an AC circuit, current can lead or lag voltage due to inductors or capacitors. 'Lead' means reaching the same cycle point earlier; 'lag' means later.\n\n## Walk through one problem\n1. A 2 Hz signal has period T = 1/2 = 0.5 s.\n2. A quarter of a cycle is 0.5/4 = **0.125 s**, corresponding to 90°.\n3. With the plus-phase convention above, +90° shifts the sine wave left (it leads). A −90° shift moves it right (lags).\n\n## Explore\nDrag the phase slider to −90°, then +90°. The wave keeps its amplitude and period. Set 90° to see its positive peak at time zero, and −90° to see its negative trough.\n\n## Watch out\nChanging frequency changes the period; changing only phase does not. A phase difference only remains fixed when both signals share the same frequency.", "v(t) = A sin(2πt/T + φ)\nT = 0.5 s ; 90° = quarter-cycle = 0.125 s", NumericExercise("Wave practice", "A waveform repeats every 0.8 seconds. A phase difference of 90° corresponds to how many seconds?", 0.2d, 0.001d, "s", "Use the worked steps and keep the sign and units.", "90° is one quarter of 360°. Time shift = 0.8 s × 90/360 = 0.2 s."), null, ElectricalFoundationsDocs),
            new("ee-phasors", "Components and practical circuits", 1340, "Phasors: connecting phase to AC circuits", "Use rotating arrows to reason about AC voltage and current.", "A phasor encodes the amplitude and phase of a sinusoid at a shared frequency; relative angle predicts leading or lagging signals.", "## Learn the idea\nA **phasor** is an arrow used to represent a sine-wave signal's **amplitude** and **phase** when its frequency is understood. Arrow length represents magnitude; arrow angle represents its phase relative to a reference signal. All phasors in one steady-state circuit diagram normally use the same frequency, so we can compare their phase angles directly rather than redrawing entire waveforms.\n\n## Prerequisites\nA wave has amplitude A, period T and phase φ. One cycle is 360° = 2π radians. The **RMS** (root-mean-square) value of a sine is its peak amplitude divided by √2. Circuit analysis often uses RMS phasor magnitudes; a waveform visualizer might use peak magnitude instead. Label which one is being used.\n\n## Where you'll use it\nA resistor's ideal sinusoidal voltage and current are in phase. In an ideal inductor, current **lags** voltage by 90°; in an ideal capacitor, current **leads** voltage by 90°. This is the foundation for AC **impedance** (generalized resistance combining magnitude and phase), power factor and motor/electrical power calculations.\n\n## Walk through one problem\n1. Choose voltage at 0° as the phase reference.\n2. An ideal inductor makes current lag by 90°. The current phasor is therefore at **−90°** relative to voltage.\n3. On the waveform graph, compare the zero-phase reference (dashed) with the phase-shifted line (green). Set phase to −90° to see the lag.\n4. If the current sine wave has peak magnitude 5 A, its instantaneous current at time zero is i(0) = 5 sin(−90°) = **−5 A**. That negative sign describes direction relative to the chosen reference, not a mistake.\n\n## Explore\nChange amplitude, period, and phase independently. Use the arrow diagram to connect angle to the green waveform. This phasor lesson remains part of the required main course, not an optional detour.\n\n## Watch out\nA phasor is **not** a claim that electrons literally trace circles in the wire. It is a mathematical representation of steady sinusoidal signals. Do not apply one phasor to a mixture of unrelated frequencies without separating the frequencies first.", "Reference voltage: 0°\nInductor current: −90° (lags voltage)\ni(t) = 5 sin(2πt/T − 90°)\ni(0) = −5 A", NumericExercise("Wave practice", "An ideal inductive current is represented by i(t) = 4 sin(2πt/T − 90°) A. What is i(0) relative to the chosen direction?", -4d, 0.001d, "A", "Use the worked steps and keep the sign and units.", "At t = 0, the argument is −90°. sin(−90°) = −1, so i(0) = 4 × (−1) = −4 A. The minus indicates opposite instantaneous direction."), null, ElectricalFoundationsDocs),
            new("ee-diode-circuit-applications",
                "Components and practical circuits",
                1450,
                "Diodes in actual circuits: polarity, flyback and detection",
                "Learn diode types, orientation, voltage drop, current ratings and real design trade-offs.",
                "A diode steers current direction; selection depends on voltage, current and switching behavior.",
                "Diodes have an anode and a cathode (marked by a band on many physical parts). With a normal forward bias, conventional current flows from anode toward cathode. With reverse bias, it blocks significant current unless its maximum reverse voltage or other limits are exceeded. Common silicon signal diodes (such as 1N4148) suit small fast signals; rectifiers (such as the 1N400x family) often handle greater low-frequency load current; Schottky diodes have different forward drop, leakage and reverse-voltage characteristics. Check the actual datasheet instead of assuming parts are interchangeable.\n\nApplications: a series diode can prevent current when a DC supply's polarity is reversed, but its forward drop wastes some voltage and power. A reverse-protection circuit might use a MOSFET instead for lower losses. Across a DC relay coil, a flyback diode is installed so it is reverse-biased while the coil is powered, then conducts when the coil switches off to limit inductive kickback. In signal encoding, a series diode can make a switch conduct in only one polarity; another switch can short the same two wires. That behavior could permit multiple commands over a two-wire connection, depending on the receiver's scanning scheme.\n\nBench test: use the multimeter diode mode across an isolated small signal diode in both orientations, record its forward-voltage reading and OL reverse reading. Ordinary ohms mode may not forward-bias a diode sufficiently. Treat diode-test voltage as a measurement, not a fixed resistor value.",
                "Signal switch A: wire 1 -- SW_A -------- wire 2\nSignal switch B: wire 1 -- SW_B --|>|-- wire 2\n\nDC coil clamp (simplified):\n+V --+-- relay coil --+-- controlled switch -- 0 V\n     +--|<|---------+   (cathode faces +V)",
                StudyExercise("Design notebook", "Use these practical checkpoints before moving to the next build or module.", new[] { "Locate the diode cathode", "Explain flyback diode orientation", "Differentiate a diode's voltage drop from resistance" }),
                null,
                ElectricalFoundationsDocs),
            new("ee-component-selection-project",
                "Components and practical circuits",
                1480,
                "Components assessment: protect an LED and relay",
                "Combine resistor, diode, capacitor and coil understanding in a design review.",
                "Every component is selected for a reason and verified against the circuit's limits.",
                "Before answering, review a complete low-voltage interface: an LED needs a current-limiting resistor; a mechanical relay coil is inductive and typically needs suitable flyback suppression when switched by a transistor; a 100 nF bypass capacitor near a digital device helps stabilize its local supply. A resistor's wattage rating must exceed its calculated dissipation with margin. A relay's coil voltage and coil current are separate from its contact voltage/current ratings. Its contacts do not automatically share electrical ground with the control electronics.\n\nDesign review walkthrough: list supply, input/output voltages, peak currents, coil voltage, LED forward voltage, diode type/polarity and all expected meter readings. Check that a 3.3 V GPIO is never exposed to an unknown 12–24 V controller line. Put any relay between the GPIO and external contact circuit only if the relay driver and power supply are appropriately designed.",
                "LED path: supply -> series R -> LED -> 0 V\nRelay driver: GPIO -> transistor driver -> relay coil\nFlyback: reverse-biased diode across DC coil\nContacts: electrically isolated switch pair",
                ChoiceExercise("Module assessment", "When a transistor turns off an ordinary DC relay coil, what is the primary purpose of a properly oriented flyback diode?", "clamp", new Choice[] { new("clamp", "Give inductor current a safe path and limit switch-off voltage spikes"), new("power", "Increase the coil's supply voltage"), new("replace", "Replace the need for a properly rated driver transistor") }, "An energized coil stores energy in its magnetic field.", "Applies module understanding safely"),
                null,
                ElectricalFoundationsDocs),
            new("ee-two-wire-encoded-buttons",
                "Control interfaces and build projects",
                1550,
                "Two wires, several buttons: encoding commands with components",
                "Distinguish direct contacts, diodes, resistor ladders and capacitor-based signals.",
                "Two conductors can carry multiple commands when the controller distinguishes different electrical signatures.",
                "A receiver is not limited to measuring whether two wires are connected. It can apply a test signal and observe the response: a direct contact may short the lines in either polarity; a series diode may conduct in only one; a series resistor may draw one of several distinguishable currents; a capacitor can produce a transient during a voltage change without offering steady DC continuity. More sophisticated controllers may use digital signaling on the same wires. The correct interpretation depends on the actual controller design.\n\nProcedure for an *isolated, low-voltage, disconnected* wall-control assembly: label both wires. With neither button pressed, use Ω mode and record the result. Hold each button and record resistance in both probe orientations. Then use diode mode in both orientations. If the meter supports capacitance, carefully measure only a safely discharged, isolated capacitive branch according to its instructions. A reading of OL can mean an open switch, a capacitor after charging, a reverse-biased diode or another component; it does not prove a specific circuit type. A near-zero Ω reading usually indicates a closed conductive contact path.\n\nBefore replacement, inspect the board and component markings and find the opener's model-specific documentation. Some garage wall controls have more complex interfaces than a direct short plus a diode. Do not directly connect a microcontroller GPIO to unknown opener terminals. The opener, door movement and safety sensors form a safety-critical system; keep the original protection systems intact.",
                "Path 1: wire A -- pushbutton 1 ------------- wire B\nPath 2: wire A -- pushbutton 2 -- resistor ---- wire B\nPath 3: wire A -- pushbutton 3 -- diode ------ wire B\nPath 4: wire A -- pushbutton 4 -- capacitor -- wire B\nThese are ALTERNATIVES, not an asserted Craftsman pinout.",
                StudyExercise("Design notebook", "Use these practical checkpoints before moving to the next build or module.", new[] { "Compare expected meter signatures", "Reverse leads in diode-test mode", "Do not infer model-specific wiring from OL alone" }),
                null,
                ElectricalFoundationsDocs),
            new("ee-custom-wall-control-project",
                "Control interfaces and build projects",
                1670,
                "Project: design replacement garage wall-control buttons",
                "Preserve original wall-control electronics while changing the mechanical interface.",
                "Replicate verified switch contacts instead of guessing how a door-opener controller decodes its wall signals.",
                "Scope: a local, supervised low-voltage replacement wall control that leaves the garage door opener, its travel limits, obstruction detection and photoelectric safety sensors unchanged. A safe approach is to retain the existing working wall-control circuit board and replace only its worn switch actuators with suitable normally-open momentary contacts. This avoids assuming the original board uses a particular resistor, capacitor, diode, voltage or communication protocol.\n\nStep 1: identify the opener and wall-control model, obtain its manufacturer manual and photos of both board sides. Step 2: disconnect the opener from mains power before inspecting the wall control and never work inside the powered opener. Identify the button contact pads on the isolated wall-control board with continuity testing; do not bridge unidentified terminals. Step 3: select momentary pushbuttons with appropriate contact ratings and physical mounting, then verify with the board disconnected that each new switch opens and closes only its intended contact pair. Step 4: mount the retained board securely in a nonconductive enclosure with strain relief, protected connections and button spacing that prevents accidental activation. Keep the door button clearly labeled and accessible.\n\nAfter careful reassembly, test under supervised conditions, with the door fully visible and the manufacturer's safety functions operating. Do not override safety sensors, bypass interlocks, or enable unattended door operation. If the old board is damaged or unidentifiable, use a compatible listed replacement wall station instead of improvising the opener interface.",
                "New DOOR button --- isolated contact pads for original DOOR switch\nNew LIGHT button -- isolated contact pads for original LIGHT switch\nRetained original wall-control PCB -- existing wall-control connection\n(No guessed controller pinout or direct GPIO connection)",
                StudyExercise("Design notebook", "Use these practical checkpoints before moving to the next build or module.", new[] { "Use original circuit as the verified interface", "Test only isolated switch contact pads", "Preserve all garage-door safety functions" }),
                null,
                ElectricalFoundationsDocs),
            new("ee-controller-isolated-relay-project",
                "Control interfaces and build projects",
                1680,
                "Project: add an Arduino or Pi behind isolated controls",
                "Plan a display and electronic button actuation without exposing GPIO to unknown signals.",
                "A relay's isolated normally-open contact can imitate a verified low-voltage button closure.",
                "Architecture: local display and buttons → microcontroller → protected relay-driver module → isolated normally-open relay contacts → known wall-station switch pads. The GPIO pin controls the relay driver only; it does not touch the garage wall-control's unknown electrical levels. A relay coil may draw more current than the GPIO can provide, so use a rated relay module or a transistor driver, a suitable DC coil supply and correct flyback protection. Confirm the relay contact pair is genuinely isolated from the coil and the contact rating suits the original switch circuit. Some 'relay boards' are active-low; a floating or booting pin can trigger them unexpectedly.\n\nStart with a bench mockup: have the controller momentarily close a relay contact across an isolated low-voltage LED test circuit, not the door opener. Add a hardware pull resistor and fail-safe startup logic so power-up, reset, crashes and reconnects cannot unexpectedly close the relay. Add independent local mechanical door and light buttons that still work when the computer is off. A separate safe door-position sensor may feed the display, but that sensor is not a substitute for the opener's built-in safety system.\n\nOnly after the interface has been identified and independently reviewed should you consider supervised integration. For remote or unattended garage-door control, a manufacturer-supported listed controller with required warnings and safety features is preferable. Do not remotely actuate a door based solely on a software display state.",
                "Pi/Arduino GPIO -> protected driver -> RELAY COIL (separate circuit)\n                                    [isolated contact pair]\nExisting door-button pad A --- COM   NO --- pad B\n\nBoot state: relay open; reset state: relay open\nHardware pushbuttons: independent of the Pi/Arduino",
                StudyExercise("Design notebook", "Use these practical checkpoints before moving to the next build or module.", new[] { "Separate GPIO and wall-station electrical domains", "Ensure startup cannot energize the relay", "Bench-test low-voltage mockup before integration" }),
                null,
                ElectricalFoundationsDocs),
            new("ee-final-project-check",
                "Control interfaces and build projects",
                1690,
                "Final assessment: choose a safe interface",
                "Apply component behavior and meter reasoning to a realistic two-wire control.",
                "Verify before connecting: neither OL nor a plausible schematic is a substitute for measurements.",
                "A two-wire wall station has two worn buttons. On the disconnected assembly one press gives near-zero Ω while the other gives OL in ordinary resistance mode. That is enough to say the observed conductive path differs; it does *not* prove a diode. A reverse-biased diode, capacitor or active circuitry might also cause OL. The correct next steps are inspection, model-specific documentation and safe isolated measurements in additional meter modes.\n\nA replacement can often preserve the manufacturer's board and reproduce verified switch contact behavior. A future computer-controlled panel can actuate those same verified contacts with an appropriately rated isolated dry-contact relay, but hardware and software must fail open and the garage door safety systems remain operational. Be able to explain what quantity your meter is measuring, where energy comes from in Ω mode and why measuring resistance on a powered opener is unsafe.",
                "Observation: OPEN in Ω mode ≠ proof of diode\nNext: power off, isolate, reverse probes, use diode mode,\ninspect board & datasheet, retain wall-control electronics",
                ChoiceExercise("Module assessment", "A light button reads OL with the switch disconnected in Ω mode. What is the best conclusion?", "uncertain", new Choice[] { new("uncertain", "The signature is not yet identified; inspect and test other modes safely"), new("diode", "It definitely contains a diode"), new("dead", "It definitely means the button is broken") }, "Several different components can appear open to an ordinary resistance meter.", "Applies module understanding safely"),
                null,
                ElectricalFoundationsDocs)
        };
}
